WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:03.750
ANJALI SASTRY: Part
of our challenge

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:08.670
as teachers is to help make
sure that we're embedding

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:11.220
into their approach
enough rigor,

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:14.430
that we're looking at
the data and the evidence

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:16.590
and that it's being linked
to the content we're

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:17.420
teaching here.

00:00:20.502 --> 00:00:21.960
SARAH HANSEN: Today
in the podcast,

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:25.530
we're looking at a new
kind of independent study.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:28.440
ANJALI SASTRY: Teaching this
way is incredibly rewarding

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.480
and also really scary.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.840
You'll often be
invited into domains

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.960
where you don't
have the expertise,

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.760
and it's quite
hard to predict how

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:43.518
a given session or
conversation will go.

00:00:43.518 --> 00:00:45.060
SARAH HANSEN: Welcome
to Chalk Radio,

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:48.090
a podcast about inspired
teaching at MIT.

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:53.850
I'm your host, Sarah Hansen,
from MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:55.530
In this episode,
we'll be talking

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:58.620
about action learning
and an exciting evolution

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:00.680
of the independent study.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:02.910
The MIT Sloan
School of Management

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:06.480
has a tradition of involving
MBA Fellows in collaborations

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.940
with partners from
around the globe.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.460
For years, these projects
have helped participants build

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.680
experience and connections.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.020
And these students,
the MBA Fellows,

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.780
are often already
established in a career

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.210
or in building
their own projects.

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:24.030
My guest today,
Dr. Anjali Sastry,

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:28.650
has led over 100 groups of
MBA Fellows in these projects.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:30.900
Now the thing to note here
is that these projects were

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.750
developed by the
school and instructors,

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:36.420
and students would sign
on to work on them.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.480
But Dr. Sastry wanted to
get students more invested

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.730
right from the
start, so she decided

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:44.500
to do something a
little different.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:48.150
We'll pick up with Dr.
Sastry's explanation.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:50.820
ANJALI SASTRY: This last
year, I tried a new experiment

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:54.870
where, instead of us
bringing in the projects,

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:58.530
I had students work with
me to create the projects

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:03.060
they were interested in that
spoke to societal challenges

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:05.220
that would be important
in the coming decade

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:08.190
and that also linked to
technology in some way.

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:10.710
Students come in with
a passion or a set

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:13.800
of questions they're really
interested in investigating.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.540
Could we take some
of the things that

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.210
work in action learning,
traditional action learning

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:23.100
classes, and bend them
towards that kind of drive?

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:27.330
Could I use my own
connections, relationships,

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:30.690
Rolodex to help students
follow their passions

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:33.660
but also to find thought
partners or project

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.650
partners in the
world and also tap

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:42.270
into my own expertise and
that of other MIT faculty

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:43.622
as well as their peers?

00:02:43.622 --> 00:02:45.330
SARAH HANSEN: Could
you talk a little bit

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:48.870
about some of the specific
projects that students did?

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:51.300
ANJALI SASTRY: The projects
students did ranged greatly,

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:55.110
but all had as a theme using
what they were learning here

00:02:55.110 --> 00:03:00.900
and tapping into emerging or
existing technology in new ways

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:05.420
to find solutions to
problems that face many.

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:11.130
One of my students, Alini,
came to MIT completely on fire

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:15.510
with this idea of tapping
into analytics and AI

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:19.170
to solve what she saw
as a major challenge

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:23.130
when it came to bringing
finance to smallholder

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:25.110
and small-scale farmers.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:27.750
She's an expert on
agricultural finance.

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:31.920
And she realized that in her
native Brazil and elsewhere,

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:35.610
people who didn't have good
credit standing and good credit

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:39.270
records, farmers who
didn't have those assets,

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:43.470
couldn't get loans and
were forever locked out.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:46.920
So there's a big dichotomy
between the people who have

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:49.920
access to finance
and those who don't.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:53.220
Could she break down
that barrier in some way?

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:57.940
Could new forms of data and
technology help do that?

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:01.530
So instead of relying only
on somebody's credit record,

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:05.910
could you look at the weather,
their farming choices,

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:09.330
their behaviors,
and physical data

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:11.520
and use it to come
up with a better

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:15.360
assessment of the riskiness of
a given farmer from the lender's

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:16.410
point of view?

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:19.380
Slightly technical
area, but it actually

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.870
has huge implications
because it could

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:24.510
open the door to
all kinds of folks

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:28.350
who are excluded from
traditional finance

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:31.650
by providing other
methods of sorting out

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:35.620
their riskiness and
their credit worthiness.

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:40.740
So she's got drone data, map and
weather data, behavioral data,

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:44.160
transactional data, and
is trying to stitch it

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:45.255
all up in new ways.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.080
SARAH HANSEN: Each
of these projects

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:53.190
required significant time and
engagement from Dr. Sastry.

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:55.980
I couldn't help but ask
how this all worked.

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:57.660
What did it look
like to be involved

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:00.690
with each student's project?

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:02.700
ANJALI SASTRY: Each
project was customized.

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:04.410
And one of the
things I've learned

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:07.440
about trying to innovate
when it comes to teaching

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.300
is you have to be
willing to invest

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:12.150
a lot the first few rounds,
the first few years you

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:12.960
do something.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:14.760
And then over
time, you learn how

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:19.380
to make it a little more
efficient, streamlined,

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:23.520
and maybe scalable and
more cost-effective.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:25.530
I learned I had to
keep a whiteboard up

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:28.140
in my office with each
project and each person,

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:32.040
because I had over a dozen
running, to keep them straight

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:35.460
and try to remember who's doing
what so that when I saw it

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:38.190
when I came into the office,
that person would be on my mind

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:40.140
or that project
would be on my mind.

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:42.270
I do think, mundane
as it sounds,

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:46.158
I think it's also important to
have good forms and paperwork.

00:05:46.158 --> 00:05:46.950
SARAH HANSEN: Sure.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:48.408
ANJALI SASTRY:
Having people really

00:05:48.408 --> 00:05:52.470
articulate in writing
briefly what they want to do

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:55.950
and having a structured
update process

00:05:55.950 --> 00:06:00.450
really helps keep the
momentum and build the focus.

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:03.570
SARAH HANSEN: How do you measure
success in projects like this?

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:05.153
ANJALI SASTRY: That's
a great question

00:06:05.153 --> 00:06:10.140
because students who are
following their passions really

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:11.320
get into it.

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:13.440
You give them
enough freedom, they

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.277
will have a good time, right?

00:06:15.277 --> 00:06:16.110
SARAH HANSEN: Right.

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:19.740
ANJALI SASTRY: But part of our
challenge as their teachers

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:24.150
is to help make sure that we're
embedding into their approach

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:28.830
enough rigor, that we're looking
at the data and the evidence

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:30.990
and that it's being linked
to the content we're

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:32.460
teaching here.

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:37.800
So a passion project by itself
may or may not be academic.

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:42.660
Not every student who comes
in with their passion project

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:45.480
is necessarily going to
want to or should turn it

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:47.970
into an in-depth study.

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:51.000
So I've learned that you
can't convert everyone.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:54.180
And that's OK because
we're offering--

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.070
this is an offering
of a new learning

00:06:56.070 --> 00:06:57.660
experience that's
very much driven

00:06:57.660 --> 00:06:59.820
by the students' interest.

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:02.820
You need to really
be able to allow

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:05.160
the students to self-select in.

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:08.700
It's hard to offer something
like this as a requirement.

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:12.480
So that's another piece is
striking the right balance

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:17.070
between opening the
door and inviting people

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:20.091
in versus cracking the whip
and following up on them.

00:07:22.615 --> 00:07:24.990
SARAH HANSEN: Dr. Sastry
pointed to one of the challenges

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:28.170
that she faced in taking on
these students' projects,

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:31.200
and it's one that many
instructors face at some point.

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:34.640
How far would she go outside
her area of expertise

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:35.640
to support her students?

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:41.960
ANJALI SASTRY: I need to be
able to figure it out for myself

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:45.350
and then also be very
clear with the students.

00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:48.530
How much do I know
about the blockchain?

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:51.240
That's not an area
of my research.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:54.980
So how far do I want to
go down a set of projects

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:56.480
that take that on?

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:57.755
I'm interested in this topic.

00:07:57.755 --> 00:08:00.950
I'll learn with you, but
my domain of expertise

00:08:00.950 --> 00:08:02.370
relates to these areas.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:04.920
We can apply it
to your questions.

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:08.930
We can apply systems thinking
or organizational change

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:12.500
or business models to the
questions you're articulating.

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:15.630
But I am not the technical
expert on this domain,

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:19.220
so you'll need to work
with someone else on that.

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:21.620
And different instructors
have different interests

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:24.250
in stepping out of
their comfort zone.

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:27.690
So I think that's
really important.

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:31.820
Another big question
is, how willing are you,

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:37.070
as an instructor, to have
a mishmash of projects that

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:39.860
take on very different domains?

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:43.220
Teaching this way is
incredibly rewarding and also

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:44.810
really scary.

00:08:44.810 --> 00:08:47.780
You'll often be
invited into domains

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:51.290
where you don't
have the expertise,

00:08:51.290 --> 00:08:53.090
and it's quite
hard to predict how

00:08:53.090 --> 00:08:56.690
a given session or a
conversation will go.

00:08:56.690 --> 00:08:59.600
It's not like running a case
where you know the story

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:03.500
and you know what you're going
to say at each moment in class

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:06.440
or you've at least got a sense
of what that might look like.

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:10.640
Because very often, students
will come in and say,

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:12.560
I've totally changed my mind.

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:13.920
And you have to deal with that.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:16.880
So there's both the
kind of personal journey

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:19.250
of learning that's
less predictable

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:22.070
and the domain part
that's less predictable.

00:09:22.070 --> 00:09:24.650
But you, as the
instructor, get to set

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:26.540
some of those parameters.

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:29.660
So I want them to
not simply make

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:32.240
general arguments about
what should happen

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:34.130
but to think about
what they could

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:38.014
do as a leader, an
entrepreneur, and executive.

00:09:41.805 --> 00:09:43.930
SARAH HANSEN: If you're
interested in learning more

00:09:43.930 --> 00:09:46.750
about social impact
technology projects

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:50.230
or independent studies, you
can find Dr. Sastry's teaching

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:54.850
materials on our
site at ocw.mit.edu.

00:09:54.850 --> 00:09:57.760
You'll also find videos
of Dr. Sastry's students

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:00.550
sharing their insights
about their own projects.

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:02.800
Additional materials and
Instructor Insights about

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:05.620
project-based learning
are on our educator portal

00:10:05.620 --> 00:10:10.150
at ocw.mit.edu/educator.

00:10:10.150 --> 00:10:13.060
As always, thank you
so much for listening.

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:15.640
We'd love for your
OCW story to become

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:17.740
a part of our conversation.

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:19.630
Use the contact link
in the show notes

00:10:19.630 --> 00:10:23.200
to share how you use OCW to
shape your own life or those

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:24.380
of others.

00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:27.100
Your story will inspire
us and other people

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:28.870
and will help us
continue to share

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:31.360
the kind of open
educational resources

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:32.980
that make a difference.

00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:38.610
Until next time, I'm Sarah
Hansen from MIT OpenCourseWare.

